{"summary": "I implemented `proverb(*inputs, qualifier=None)` in `proverb.py`. I didn't run it, and the project has no tests.

It returns a list of lines: one "For want of a X the Y was lost." for each adjacent pair of inputs. The last line is "And all for the want of a …", using the first input. If you pass `qualifier`, it goes before that first input, for example "a rusty nail". An empty input list returns an empty list.

I chose the list return and the `*inputs` signature from memory of the Exercism Python track's version of this exercise. If your hidden tests expect a single string instead of a list, the return needs changing."}
